nodecode-impossibl is a rewrite of impossiblco's impossiblco/hermes-impossibl:
https://github.com/impossiblco/hermes-impossibl
commit 146805e4eabab33687fa2e34bbe959f0057f9497
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Impossibl (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the provider profile (its base, its key variable, the
catalog row it becomes), the reading of Impossibl's `/v1/models` answer that
keeps only the models serving `/v1/chat/completions` which do not declare
`supports_tools: false`, the fallback to the core's own listing when that read
fails or names none, the cache that keeps one answered catalog for the
process, the fallback model roster, and the deferral to the generic listing
when the base is not Impossibl's own. The profile became a config section
(`base_url`, `chat_only`, `timeout_seconds`) plus three hooks: the catalog
row, the credential and the model listing. The Hermes plumbing the plugin
relied on rather than wrote -- the model picker, `/model`, `hermes doctor`,
`--provider`, the credentialed-URL opener, the credential resolution -- is the
core's here and was not rewritten. The provider id is `impossibl`, which is
what makes the core's own `<ID>_API_KEY` ladder read `IMPOSSIBL_API_KEY`.

What was left out: the profile fields Nodecode has no use for (`api_mode`,
`auth_type`, `hostname`, `signup_url` as profile data, and
`default_aux_model`, which Nodecode pins per session in its own config); the
`impossibl.com` and `impossibl-ai` aliases, since `/connect` and `/models`
name the provider by its id; `IMPOSSIBL_BASE_URL`, whose place the section's
`base_url` takes; and context windows, which the plugin left to models.dev
rather than read from the catalog -- a row carries one only when the catalog
itself states it.
